# Environment for the end-to-end build. COMMITTED ON PURPOSE.
#
# These are placeholders, not secrets, and nothing here is used to reach a real
# service. The file exists so that `npm run gate:e2e` is hermetic: it builds and
# tests the same way on a developer's machine and in CI, and it never points the
# browser at a real Supabase project.
#
# Why this file has to exist at all — the mistake it fixes:
#
# Vite inlines `import.meta.env.VITE_*` into the bundle at BUILD time, not at
# serve time. The e2e config used to pass placeholder credentials through
# Playwright's `webServer.env`, which sets variables on the *preview server
# process* — long after the values have been baked into the JavaScript. So in CI,
# where no `.env` exists, the build produced an app with no Supabase URL at all,
# `isSupabaseConfigured` was false, and the app rendered the config-error screen.
#
# The visible damage was seven failing tests. The invisible damage was worse: the
# axe assertions still *passed*, because a page with no forms, no links and no
# interactive controls has no accessibility violations. The accessibility suite
# was asserting nothing in CI, on every run, while reporting green.
#
# `vite build --mode test` loads this file with a higher priority than `.env`, so
# the e2e build is the same everywhere and cannot accidentally pick up a real
# project's credentials.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://placeholder.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_placeholder
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
